 "result_text": "I changed `Light.set_light` in `src/uiprotect/data/devices.py` so you can turn the floodlight off when Protect turned it on. I haven't run it or any tests, and I didn't reproduce the problem against a real floodlight.\n\nMy diagnosis from the code (I didn't read the linked Home Assistant issue): if Protect turns the light on (motion, for example), `is_light_on` is true but `is_led_force_on` is still false. `set_light(False)` then sets `is_led_force_on` to false, which changes nothing. With no difference to send, no update goes to Protect, so the light stays on.\n\nThe fix: when turning off a light that Protect switched on, `set_light` first sends `is_led_force_on = True` as a real update. The existing off call then has something to send.\n\nThe cost is that a \"turn off\" in this case makes two requests to Protect instead of one, and the light is briefly force-on in between.\n\nI also didn't wrap the new `if` line, so the repo's linter or formatter may want to reflow it.",
